CLAIM EXTRACTION / INFLATION MAP
================================
Target: Mann, M.E., Bradley, R.S. & Hughes, M.K. (1998). Global-scale temperature patterns and climate forcing over the past six centuries. Nature 392, 779–787. (MBH98)
URL: https://www.meteo.psu.edu/holocene/public_html/shared/articles/mbh98.pdf
Document type: academic paper
Date of map: 2026-07-30

-----------------------------------------------------------------
1. WHAT WAS LITERALLY MEASURED
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Spatially resolved annual surface temperature patterns (and derived Northern Hemisphere and global means) over AD 1400–1995, reconstructed via multivariate (EOF/PCA) calibration of a multiproxy network of annual-resolution indicators (primarily dendroclimatic/tree-ring, ice-core/ice-melt, coral, and limited historical/instrumental records) against the dominant spatiotemporal patterns in the instrumental surface temperature field (1902–1980/1995 calibration period; 1,082 grid-points). 

Network size decreases backward in time (112 indicators to ~1820; fewer earlier, down to 22–24 indicators by 1400–1450). Skill metrics (resolved variance b, verification correlations) degrade with sparser networks; large-scale NH mean retains more skill than spatial patterns before ~1600–1700. Uncertainties expand backward as Gaussian unresolved variance. Forcing correlations (GHG, solar, volcanic) performed on the NH series in moving windows.

Verification state: FULL-TEXT-CONFIRMED (from PDF text extraction of abstract, methods, results, discussion).

-----------------------------------------------------------------
2. STRONGEST CLAIM THE SOURCE ITSELF MAKES
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Abstract (unhedged):
"Northern Hemisphere mean annual temperatures for three of the past eight years are warmer than any other year since (at least) AD 1400."

Body (quantified, with uncertainty):
"Taking into account the uncertainties in our NH reconstruction (see Methods), it appears that the years 1990, 1995 and now 1997 (this value recently calculated and not shown) each show anomalies that are greater than any other year back to 1400 at 3 standard errors, or roughly a 99.7% level of certainty."

Additional strong claims:
- "The positive trend in RPC no. 1 during the twentieth century is clearly exceptional in the context of the long-term variability in the associated eigenvector, and indeed describes much of the unprecedented warming trend evident in the NH reconstruction."
- Greenhouse gases "emerging as the dominant forcing during the twentieth century" (from time-dependent correlations of the reconstruction with forcing series).

Note: Abstract and body both assert the warmest-years claim; body supplies the 3σ / 99.7% quantification and explicit years. The "unprecedented" language appears in the body description of the RPC1 trend and NH reconstruction.

Verification state: FULL-TEXT-CONFIRMED.

-----------------------------------------------------------------
3. WEAKER DEFENSIBLE CLAIM THE EVIDENCE ACTUALLY SUPPORTS
-----------------------------------------------------------------
Author reconstruction from Field 1: Under the assumptions of the multiproxy EOF calibration method (linearity, stationarity of leading modes, adequacy of the sparse network to capture large-scale variance), the reconstructed NH annual mean temperature series indicates that the years 1990, 1995 and 1997 exceed all other reconstructed years in the AD 1400–1995 interval at approximately the 3-standard-error level reported by the authors. Spatial pattern reconstructions become marginal before ~1600 and are not skilful before ~1450; the NH mean retains more skill but with expanding uncertainty bands. The 20th-century rise in the leading mode is large relative to the reconstructed variability of the preceding five centuries in this particular reconstruction. Forcing attributions are correlational and time-window dependent.

Verification state: author reconstruction from Field 1 (FULL-TEXT-CONFIRMED source text).

